## 2. Initialize and Commit in VS Code

<Sequence>

  <Step subtitle="Shortcut: Ctrl + Shift + G" title="Open the Source Control Tab">
    Look at the left-hand sidebar in VS Code and click on the **Source Control** icon (it looks like a branch with points). 
  </Step>
  <Step subtitle="Creates local Git folder" title="Initialize Repository">
    Click the blue **"Initialize Repository"** button at the top of the panel. This creates a hidden `.git` folder in your project workspace.
  </Step>
  <Step subtitle="Prepares files for tracking" title="Stage Your Project Files">
    You will see a long list of your files under the **Changes** section. Click the **`+` (plus sign)** icon next to the word "Changes" to stage all files at once. They will move up to "Staged Changes".
  </Step>
  <Step subtitle="Saves your progress locally" title="Commit Your Code">
    In the text box at the top of the panel, type a meaningful message like `feat: initial library system commit`. Then, click the blue **"Commit"** button.
  </Step>
</Sequence>

---

## 3. Publish to GitHub
Once your files are committed locally, it's time to send them to the cloud:

1. Look back at the **Source Control** panel where you just committed. 
2. You will now see a blue button that says **"Publish Branch"**. Click it.
3. VS Code will show a dropdown menu at the top of your screen asking you to choose:
   * **Publish to GitHub private repository**
   * **Publish to GitHub public repository**
4. Choose **Public repository** (since your evaluator needs to look at the link without permissions).
5. If this is your first time, a popup will ask you to authorize VS Code to access your GitHub account. Click **Allow**, sign in through your browser, and confirm.

VS Code will automatically create the repository on your GitHub profile, link your local files to it, and upload everything seamlessly. Once it finishes, a small success popup will give you a button to **"Open on GitHub"** where you can grab your link for your submission!